        "item": {
          "clientId": null,
          "content": [
            {
              "text": "{\"problem\": {\"theorem\": \"2. Show that there are infinitely many pairs of positive integers $(m, n)$ such that\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\frac{m+1}{n}+\\\\frac{n+1}{m}\\n$$\\nis a positive integer.\", \"assumptions\": [], \"domain\": \"british_mo\", \"explicit_subgoals\": []}, \"candidate_text\": \"Define integers \\\\(a_0=1\\\\), \\\\(a_1=2\\\\), and, for \\\\(k\\\\ge0\\\\),\\n\\\\[\\na_{k+2}=4a_{k+1}-a_k-1.\\n\\\\]\\nWe first show that these are positive and strictly increasing. This holds for \\\\(a_1>a_0\\\\). If \\\\(a_{k+1}>a_k\\\\ge1\\\\), then\\n\\\\[\\na_{k+2}-a_{k+1}=3a_{k+1}-a_k-1\\\\ge 2a_{k+1}>0.\\n\\\\]\\nThus induction proves \\\\(a_{k+1}>a_k>0\\\\) for every \\\\(k\\\\).\\n\\nWe next prove\\n\\\\[\\na_{k+1}^2+a_k^2+a_{k+1}+a_k-4a_{k+1}a_k=0 \\\\tag{1}\\n\\\\]\\nfor all \\\\(k\\\\ge0\\\\). It holds for \\\\((a_1,a_0)=(2,1)\\\\), since\\n\\\\[\\n2^2+1^2+2+1-4\\\\cdot2\\\\cdot1=0.\\n\\\\]\\nSuppose (1) holds. By symmetry, \\\\(a_k\\\\) is a root of\\n\\\\[\\nx^2+(1-4a_{k+1})x+(a_{k+1}^2+a_{k+1})=0.\\n\\\\]\\nThe sum of its two roots is \\\\(4a_{k+1}-1\\\\), so the other root is\\n\\\\[\\n4a_{k+1}-1-a_k=a_{k+2}.\\n\\\\]\\nConsequently (1) also holds with \\\\((a_{k+2},a_{k+1})\\\\), completing the induction.\\n\\nFor each \\\\(k\\\\ge0\\\\), set\\n\\\\[\\n(m,n)=(a_{k+1},a_k).\\n\\\\]\\nThese are positive integers, and dividing (1) by \\\\(a_{k+1}a_k\\\\) gives\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac{m+1}{n}+\\\\frac{n+1}{m}=4,\\n\\\\]\\na positive integer. Since \\\\(a_k\\\\) is strictly increasing, the pairs \\\\((a_{k+1},a_k)\\\\) are all distinct. Hence there are infinitely many required pairs of positive integers.\"}",
              "text_elements": [],
              "type": "text"
            }
          ],
          "id": "01a094c3-205b-71d3-b76a-90654d875966",
          "type": "userMessage"
